{"translation": "Erasistratus mentioned both, [570] not in an articulated way, it is true, nor as clearly as I do now, but nevertheless so as to make plain that the nature of the matter is twofold. For in saying: 'At the beginning, then, when the vessels are filled moderately, they appear more vigorous and stronger than themselves, beyond what is customary', he indicated the disproportion of the blood in excess of what is natural for each person; and in what he adds next, saying: 'but when they come further into filling, the arms and hands and shins are filled up as well', he indicated the disproportion relative to the coats that contain the blood. So there are two limits of the increase of the blood beyond what is natural: one in which the coats of the veins are filled, rising into a swelling beyond what is natural for each, and the other in which, in addition to this, it happens that they are stretched; neither of these is seen in the plethora relative to the strength. About these matters, then, this too is sufficient. Redness, which they also mention, is a sign not of plethora simply, but of sanguine plethora.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 16, "surface": "διττὴν", "english": "twofold", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 53, "surface": "ἀμετρίαν·", "english": "disproportion", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 60, "surface": "ἐπιπλέον", "english": "further", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 62, "surface": "πληρώσεως", "english": "filling", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 97, "surface": "φλεβῶν", "english": "veins", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 136, "surface": "ἔρευθος,", "english": "Redness", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 146, "surface": "αἱματικοῦ", "english": "sanguine", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 148, "surface": "γνώρισμα.", "english": "sign", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["εὐτονώτεροί τε καὶ ἰσχυρότεροι ἑαυτῶν", "παρεμπίμπλανται", "πληροῦσθαι τῶν φλεβῶν τοὺς χιτῶνας", "ἑκάστω", "ἐν τῷ πρὸς τὴν δύναμιν πλήθει"]}
